   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <gap reason="published"/>
                    <p>
                        <seg type="published" function="context">
                            <quote>I am much relieved by the immediate prospect of the <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">advance of royalties</seg>
                            </quote>
                            <bibl>LI 163</bibl>
                        </seg>
                    </p>
                    <p>and I would probably have spared myself a great deal of profitless worry <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">if I had forced</seg> <name type="person" key="hueb76">Mr Huebsch</name>’s hand three months ago. I perceived a few minutes after my meeting him that he considered me an embarrassing person and my books bad sellers but could not make up his mind to take a definite step. I may say that it is a year and a half or perhaps more since he forwarded any royalties on <rs type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">my novel</rs>, <rs type="work" key="W_EXILES">play</rs> or story. It seems strange that there should be no sales, however small. I am glad that you intend to make the arrangement you so promptly and kindly suggest without the intervention of <name type="person" key="pink62">Mr Pinker</name>. If I thought the matter was passing through his hands I should again give way to dejection.</p>           <p>
                        <seg type="published" function="context">
                            <quote>To my surprise I received a card from <name type="person" key="poun85">Mr Pound</name> who is now in Paris.</quote>
                            <bibl>LI 163</bibl>
                        </seg>
                    </p> <gap reason="published"/>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">On 10 April 1921, <rs type="letter" key="L_68756">JJ wrote to Harriet Weaver</rs> about his financial straits and needing an advance on the publication of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>. He asked her for help in arranging such an advance (<name type="work">LI</name> 162-63).</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">JJ demanded from B. W. Huebsch that he return the manuscript of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>. For further context, see <rs type="letter" key="L_68756">JJ to Weaver, 10 April 1921</rs>, notes.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
